@description REAPER MCP — Model Context Protocol interface (immersive/spatial audio)
@version 1.20.0
@author James Livingston
@link https://github.com/inseglet-audio/inseglet
@provides
  [darwin] reaper_mcp.dylib https://github.com/inseglet-audio/inseglet/releases/download/v$version/reaper_mcp.dylib
@changelog
  The true-peak reading now tells you how much it cannot see -- on BOTH sides.

  Version 1.19.0 disclosed how far the estimator's evaluation grid can read a sine BELOW the truth,
  and said in words that the interpolation filter's own error was not covered. That error has now
  been measured, against an exact reference, on the EBU's published programme material, with every
  window read in the context of its file rather than cut out of it: the estimator can also read
  ABOVE the truth, by up to 0.22 dB on Euroradio material.

  WHAT CHANGES.

  Every payload that already reported `truePeakGridBoundDb` now reports `truePeakFilterGainBoundDb`
  beside it: the most a reading can sit above the truth for a sine, computed from the filter's own
  coefficients at first call rather than written down as a number, so it cannot drift away from the
  code that produces it. For the Recommendation's table it is 0.222 dB, reached near a quarter of the
  sample rate.

  THE BOUND SHIPS WITH ITS SCOPE. It is an OVER-read bound for the INTERIOR reading
  (`truePeakDbInterior`): at a raw window edge `truePeakDb` can exceed it by the edge ring that
  `truePeakEdgeDominated` already flags. It does not bound the filter's LOSS on the low side, and it
  is derived for a sine -- a broadband transient can exceed it slightly.

  THE DESCRIPTION NOW STATES THE MEASURED WORST CASES WITH THEIR PROVENANCE, replacing the single
  figure 1.19.0 carried ("about 0.13 dB low", measured on a synthesised signal against a reference
  later shown not to have converged): the standard's own true-peak test signals read up to 0.30 dB
  low (EBU Tech 3341 case 17, inside the standard's tolerance); programme material reads up to
  0.52 dB LOW (SQAM 27, castanets) and up to 0.22 dB HIGH (Euroradio 05), all against an exact
  reference on the EBU's published files. A professional can bracket a reading from both sides and
  judge whether the tolerance meets their standard, or reach for a more accurate tool.

  `truePeakDb` itself is unchanged: the header was compiled before and after and read IDENTICAL over
  the same 12 fixtures as 1.19.0.
